    <name>Kangerlussuaq Arctic MTB (Greenland) [no lift]</name>
    <desc>Hardcore tundra riding from town toward the ice sheet

Trails
Greenland's longest connected road and surrounding rough tracks make Kangerlussuaq its most convenient mountain-bike base. The signature objective runs roughly 25 km from town toward Russell Glacier or Point 660 at the ice-sheet edge over rocky, sandy and rolling glacial terrain. This is remote adventure riding rather than gravity laps, with musk oxen and reindeer possible along the route.

Getting up / price
No lift. Ride out-and-back, rent locally or arrange a guide and vehicle support. Distances, loose surfaces, wind and a complete lack of services make even the road ride a substantial backcountry undertaking.

&amp;#9888;&amp;#65039; Carry repair equipment, emergency layers, food and communication suitable for Arctic wilderness. Keep a large distance from wildlife and never approach glacier fronts or the ice sheet without expert guidance.
Open season
Open from 15 June; closed from 15 September (conservative summer indication).
Daily opening hours: daylight only
There is no formal operating season. Snowmelt, dust, wind and rapidly changing cold weather determine whether the route is practical.

